/*
 * OVERRIDE of pico-sdk's src/rp2_common/pico_standard_link/script_include/
 * default_rodata_excludes.incl - the `.rodata` sibling of this directory's
 * default_text_excludes.incl (see that file's own long header comment for
 * the full mechanism, the reasoning, and the file-by-file justification;
 * this file exists to say only what is different).
 *
 * WHY THIS FILE EXISTS SEPARATELY, RATHER THAN LEAVING .rodata ALONE: the
 * BOOT-read hazard (docs/decisions/0004/0005) is about any core1
 * instruction FETCH racing core0's flash chip-select borrow - and
 * default_text_excludes.incl only moves *code* (`.text*`) off flash for
 * the functions core1 reaches. A function can also read separately
 * declared `static const` data (a lookup table, a struct, a string literal
 * that is not part of its own inline literal pool) that the compiler
 * places in its OWN `.rodata.<name>` input section, distinct from the
 * function's `.text.<name>` - and reading such a value while flash is
 * deselected is exactly the same class of corrupted fetch as an
 * instruction fetch would be, since both go over the same QSPI XIP path.
 * Literal-pool constants (most of what these functions actually use -
 * confirmed by inspection, not assumed: none of firmware/runtime/sensors.c
 * or tilt.c declare a `static const` on core1's path, and vendor/SDK
 * functions here are small register-twiddling routines, not table-driven
 * ones) already move WITH their owning function's `.text.<name>` section
 * automatically, because GCC with -ffunction-sections emits a Thumb
 * function's own literal pool inside that same section - this file is
 * for the OTHER case, applied uniformly rather than function-by-function,
 * since the cost is already paid (this file's own text-side twin already
 * moved these same files' code) and re-auditing every function for
 * separately-declared const data by hand is exactly the kind of manual
 * check this project's own invariant-checker philosophy (decisions 0004
 * and 0006) argues against relying on.
 *
 * SAME FILE LIST AS default_text_excludes.incl, for the same reasons -
 * see that file for what each one is and why it is reachable from core1.
 * Not narrowed to "only the files that are known to declare const data" on
 * purpose: tools/invariants rule 1 checks CODE placement only (its own
 * "what this cannot do" is that it does not model data fetches at all,
 * per this project's `bx <reg>` and veneer disclosures) and cannot tell
 * this file's author which of these files' functions read a rodata symbol
 * outside their own literal pool, so this errs toward covering all of
 * them rather than trusting a partial, unverified answer.
 */
*(EXCLUDE_FILE(
    *libgcc.a:
    *libc.a:
    *lib*_a-mem*.o
    *libm.a:
    *libg.a:
    *hardware_claim/claim.c.obj
    *hardware_sync/sync.c.obj
    *hardware_sync_spin_lock/sync_spin_lock.c.obj
    *hardware_irq/irq.c.obj
    *hardware_exception/exception.c.obj
    *hardware_timer/timer.c.obj
    *pico_time/time.c.obj
    *pico_sync/lock_core.c.obj
    *pico_sync/mutex.c.obj
    *pico_flash/flash.c.obj
    *pico_multicore/multicore.c.obj
    *pico_runtime/runtime.c.obj
    *pico_runtime_init/runtime_init.c.obj
    *pico_thread_local/thread_local.c.obj
    *pico_bootrom/bootrom.c.obj
    *pico_clib_interface/newlib_interface.c.obj
    *pico_float/float_sci_m33_vfp.S.obj
    *pico_platform_panic/panic.c.obj
    *pico_printf/printf.c.obj
    *pico_stdio/stdio.c.obj
    *libTouch.a:
) .rodata*)
